# name: test/sql/integration/unary_error_propagation.test
# description: A worker-raised error in a UNARY RPC reaches SQL with the worker's
#   own message, on every transport.
# group: [vgi_integration]

# Errors raised inside a *stream* are covered by table/generator_exception.test:
# they ride as metadata on a batch, and the message survives. A error raised in a
# UNARY call — `bind` is the one every query makes — travels a different path
# entirely: there is no stream to carry it, so the server has to answer the
# request itself with an error payload. That path had no test.
#
# It is also the path that is easiest to lose over HTTP, because HTTP already
# has a way to say "failed" that carries nothing: a status code. If the server's
# request handling lets an exception escape the RPC layer, the framework
# underneath answers with its own error page, the client sees `HTTP 500` with no
# Arrow body, and the worker's type and message survive only in the server's log.
# Subprocess transport cannot regress that way — there is no status code to fall
# back to — so this file is only meaningful when it runs on BOTH transports:
#   make test_launcher
#   ./test/run_http_integration.sh test/sql/integration/unary_error_propagation.test
#
# The assertions are on substrings the worker itself authored (a predicate name,
# a constraint phrase). A transport-level failure cannot contain them, which is
# what makes this a test of propagation rather than of failure.
#
# Scope, so the next reader does not over-trust this file: it covers errors the
# worker's *method* raises. It cannot cover errors raised while the server is
# still validating the request, because this client only ever sends well-formed
# requests — reaching that path takes a deliberately malformed one, which SQL
# has no way to express. (vgi-rpc-python let such an error escape its RPC layer
# entirely; that is regressed from a client that can malform a request, in
# vgi-java's PythonWorkerHttpConformanceTest.)

require-env VGI_TEST_WORKER

require vgi

require httpfs

statement ok
ATTACH 'example' AS example (TYPE vgi, LOCATION '${VGI_TEST_WORKER}');

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scalar bind. `double` accepts any numeric type and rejects the rest, so the
# rejection is worker-authored: nothing in the client or the transport decides
# that a string is unacceptable here.
#
# The assertion is the TAG, not the prose. This used to require the message to
# contain `_is_multipliable_type` — the Python fixture's private predicate
# symbol, reached because that SDK's framework renders a failed type bound as
# "column type string does not match any of: <predicate names>". No other
# worker can satisfy it: TypeScript says "Unsupported numeric type for
# addition: Utf8", and there is no common substring between them, not even the
# type name (`string` vs `Utf8`). Every `.test` here runs against all five
# SDKs, so an assertion only one can pass is a cross-SDK failure dressed up as
# coverage.
#
# What IS contractual is whose error this is. The C++ side tags an error that
# came back from the worker distinctly from one it raised itself about the
# worker, so a transport failure would surface as the extension's own and fail
# this. To also assert the REASON, the fixtures would first have to agree on a
# token to put in the message — a fixture-parity change across five repos, not
# a test change.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement error
SELECT example.main.double('abc');
----
VGI Worker Exception

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Table-function bind. A different unary bind path (table, not scalar) and a
# different kind of worker-side rejection (argument constraint, not schema).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement error
SELECT * FROM example.main.sequence(10, batch_size := 0);
----
must be >= 1

statement error
SELECT * FROM example.main.sequence(10, batch_size := 0);
----
VGI Worker Exception

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The failures above must leave the attachment usable. A worker that answered
# an error by tearing down the connection would pass every assertion so far and
# still be broken; over a pooled transport it would also poison the next query
# to reuse that worker.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

query I
SELECT example.main.double(21);
----
42

query I
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM example.main.sequence(10);
----
10

# And an error is still reported correctly *after* a success — the pooled worker
# is reused by then, so this is not a repeat of the first assertion.
statement error
SELECT * FROM example.main.sequence(10, batch_size := 0);
----
must be >= 1

query I
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM example.main.sequence(5);
----
5

statement ok
DETACH example;
